1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:06,119
This is infer: an LLM inference server I built from scratch in PyTorch.

2
00:00:06,469 --> 00:00:15,768
It serves Qwen2.5-0.5B with its own forward pass, and I measured it against vLLM on the same GPU.

3
00:00:16,918 --> 00:00:19,094
Two threads split the work.

4
00:00:19,444 --> 00:00:26,543
The API side runs on an asyncio event loop: it handles HTTP, tokenization and streaming.

5
00:00:26,893 --> 00:00:34,723
The engine thread owns the GPU. Every step, the scheduler decides who prefills, who decodes and who is preempted.

6
00:00:35,073 --> 00:00:40,040
Then one forward pass serves them all, reading and writing a paged KV cache.

7
00:00:41,190 --> 00:00:45,625
Here it is end to end, running on this laptop's CPU.

8
00:00:45,974 --> 00:00:49,756
The server loads the weights and sizes its KV cache.

9
00:00:50,906 --> 00:00:56,584
A plain curl request streams tokens back as server-sent events, in OpenAI's format.

10
00:00:58,763 --> 00:01:04,353
Because the API is OpenAI-compatible, the official Python client works unchanged.

11
00:01:09,093 --> 00:01:15,128
The server exports Prometheus metrics: requests, KV usage and tokens.

12
00:01:16,278 --> 00:01:23,387
Now eight requests at once. They share every forward pass, so every stream advances on every step.

13
00:01:31,779 --> 00:01:36,746
On a T4 GPU, the same server streams fifty requests at once.

14
00:01:37,096 --> 00:01:45,160
Forty-nine share each forward pass, the KV cache stays under one percent full, and all fifty finish in about seven seconds.

15
00:01:46,310 --> 00:01:51,813
The benchmarks ran for about seventeen GPU hours on Kaggle, three runs per point.

16
00:01:52,163 --> 00:02:01,515
Continuous batching delivers 3.7 times the throughput of static batching, and the paged KV cache fits 17 times more sequences in the same memory.

17
00:02:02,665 --> 00:02:09,129
With the same number of users on each server, it reaches 52 to 59 percent of vLLM's throughput.

18
00:02:10,279 --> 00:02:22,505
The profiler shows where the rest goes: at batch 32, the GPU sits idle 85 of 96 milliseconds per step, waiting for Python to launch about 1,250 small kernels.

19
00:02:22,855 --> 00:02:29,982
CUDA graphs replay the whole step with one launch, cutting batch-one decode from 27 to 10 milliseconds per token.

20
00:02:31,132 --> 00:02:41,914
But graphs only help while launches dominate; at 128 users they were slower. The final design uses them up to batch 32, and wins at every load.

21
00:02:43,064 --> 00:02:53,360
Every design choice was settled by a measurement: decode-first or prefill-first, chunked prefill, prefix caching, recompute or swap.

22
00:02:54,510 --> 00:03:04,048
And every speed-up had to keep the golden test passing: fifty prompts, sixty-four tokens each, identical to HuggingFace in fp32.

23
00:03:04,398 --> 00:03:11,466
A thirty-minute soak test completed all 5,580 requests, with no memory drift.

24
00:03:12,616 --> 00:03:16,251
Half the bugs I found were in the measuring, not the server.

25
00:03:17,401 --> 00:03:21,972
The code, the tests, and every raw result are in the repository.

